\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{The turn in an ordered transverse plane}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Take #{n=3}, #{c=3}, equal grid periods on axes one and two,
and a free axial label with #{f_3(r)\ne0}. The transverse plane
is ordered #{(1,2)}. Apply \ref{fdip-017B} to a joint eigenvector
in the volume sign #{s} with eigenvalue #{\xi}. On this vector
#{\varepsilon_1\varepsilon_2} has eigenvalue #{\ui s b}, where
#{b=\operatorname{sgn}(\xi)\operatorname{sgn}(f_3(r))}.
Thus the turn has eigenvalue
##{\frac{1+\ui s b}{\sqrt{2}}=\zeta^{s b},
\qquad \zeta=\frac{1+\ui}{\sqrt{2}}.}
The equal periods permit this coordinate turn, and its plane
fixes the axial label. When the group of \ref{fdip-016U} is
represented by the supplied lifts, this is the degree #{s b}
modulo #{8} for the chosen generator #{u_{12}}. Reversing the
plane replaces the generator by its inverse.}

\p{At #{n=5} with #{c=5}, the transverse product in
\ref{fdip-017B} contains both #{\varepsilon_1\varepsilon_2}
and #{\varepsilon_3\varepsilon_4}. The two factors commute
and each splits into eigenspaces at #{\pm\ui}, so they have
joint eigenvectors. Applying #{\varepsilon_1\varepsilon_3}
negates both eigenvalues and preserves their product. It
also preserves the axial direction and the volume sign.
Thus the product eigenvalue cannot select the multiplier
of the first plane turn, even when its periods agree.}
